Project idea

A lightweight neighborhood resource map with shared updates, helpful links, and volunteer opportunities.


Purpose
The project would help residents find practical local information without requiring them to search through scattered social posts, outdated websites, or long message threads. A visitor could open the map, browse resources by category, and quickly see what is available nearby.

Core features
- A map and searchable list of community resources.
- Categories for food, health, transport, learning, events, and mutual aid.
- Short descriptions with opening times, contact details, and source links.
- A clear way to suggest a new resource or report an outdated entry.
- A lightweight review queue for checking changes before publication.

The first release should stay intentionally small. It could begin with one neighborhood and a curated collection of entries. This would make it easier to test the language, navigation, and maintenance process before inviting a wider audience.


Possible user experience
Someone looking for a community pantry could select the food category, filter by distance, and open an entry with directions and visiting details. A volunteer could submit a new listing through a short form. A moderator could then confirm the source, publish the listing, and set a reminder to check it later.

Implementation notes
The map should work well on a phone and should not depend on a high-speed connection. Data could begin in a simple structured file or small database, with an upgrade path if usage grows. Each entry should include a last-reviewed date so users can judge how current it is.

Success measures
The pilot would be successful if residents can find a relevant resource in under two minutes, contributors understand how to submit updates, and moderators can review changes without a complicated workflow. Future versions might add multilingual labels, saved resources, partner dashboards, and public data exports.